\fs6\
\pard \s39 \li2520 \fi-604 \ri1007 \ql \f1 \b0 \fs28 + (void)\b setCellClass:\b0 (Class)\i factoryId\i0  \
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Sets the default class that the NSMatrix class will use to make cells. \i factoryId\i0  should be the \b id\b0  of a subclass of NSCell (usually NSActionCell), obtained by sending the \b class\b0  message to either the NSCell subclass object or to an instance of that subclass. The default NSCell class is NSActionCell.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Your code should rarely need to invoke this method, since each instance of NSMatrix can be configured to use its own NSCell class (or a prototype that gets copied). The NSCell class set with this method is simply a fall-back for matrices initialized with \b initWithFrame:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\pard \s58 \li2804 \fi-1292 \ri1007 \ql \tx2799 \tx3282 \tx3771 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 See also:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setCellClass:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b setPrototype:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b makeCellAtRow:column:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b initWithFrame:mode:cellClass:numberOfRows:numberOfColumns:\b0 , \f3 -\f1 \'80\b initWithFrame:mode:prototype:numberOfRows:numberOfColumns:\
